11.5 Taphonomic and ecological effects on fossils
Now we can apply our ecological and taphonomic filters to the fossil object:

fossils_pres = fossils |&gt;
  admtools::rev_dir(ref = t_max) |&gt;
  StratPal::apply_niche(niche_def = niche_def,
                        gc = gradient) |&gt;
  StratPal::apply_taphonomy(pres_potential = gap_remove,
                            ctc = identity) |&gt;
  admtools::rev_dir(ref = t_max)
plot(fossils_pres, tree)








Note that we can combine the original fossil sampling rate psi with the ecological and taphonomic effects to determine the continuously varying sampling rate in time:

total_sampling_rate = function(x){
  y = psi * niche_def(gradient(x)) * gap_remove(x)
  return(y)
}
# use lower temporal resolution
t_low_res = seq(t_min, t_max, length.out = 100)
plot(x = t_low_res,
     y = total_sampling_rate(t_low_res),
     type = "l",
     xlab = "Time",
     ylab = "Effective sampling rate")








Often, the original sampling rate psi needs to be chosen high to make sure any fossils are preserved. If you would like to subsample your fossils to a fixed small number, you can use the following function for it. We subsample to 30 fossils to reduce runtime in the inference later.

subsample_fossils = function(fossils, n){
  #' randomly select a number of fossils
  ind = sample(seq_len(length(fossils$sp)), size = n)
  return(fossils[ind,])
}
fossils_used = fossils_pres |&gt;
  subsample_fossils(n = 30)

11.7 Modify ages
Now we can modify the ages stored in the fossil object:

fossils_age_misassigned = morpho_mkv$fossil |&gt; 
  admtools::rev_dir(ref = t_max) |&gt;
  admtools::time_to_strat(adm) |&gt; 
  admtools::strat_to_time(adm_assumed) |&gt;
  admtools::rev_dir(ref = t_max)
# replace correct ages with misassigned ages
# for easier export via MorphSim::write.morpho
morpho_mkv$fossil = fossils_age_misassigned

Do you know why this is only done now and not in one of the earlier steps that modify the fossils?
